Day 25 – OAuth2 + Refresh Tokens 🔑

OAuth2 is the industry-standard protocol for authorization, widely used in APIs, microservices, and web/mobile apps. Refresh tokens complement OAuth2 to enable long-lived sessions securely.

1️⃣ What is OAuth2?

OAuth2 is a framework for delegated authorization, allowing one application to access resources on behalf of a user, without exposing user credentials.

Key Roles:

Role	Description
Resource Owner	User who owns the data
Client	Application requesting access
Authorization Server	Issues access/refresh tokens
Resource Server	Hosts the protected resource/API
OAuth2 is an authorization framework that allows an application to access a user’s resources on another service without sharing the user’s password.

Instead of giving credentials to every application, the user grants permission using tokens.

Simple Definition

OAuth2 is a protocol that allows third-party applications to access user data securely on behalf of the user, using access tokens instead of passwords.

Why OAuth2 is Used

OAuth2 solves a major security problem:

Without OAuth2:

User → gives username & password to third-party app

Risk:

Password exposure

Third-party app could misuse credentials

With OAuth2:

User → Authorizes access → Access Token issued

The app never sees the password.

Example (Google Login)

When you click “Login with Google”:

1️⃣ App redirects to Google login
2️⃣ User logs in to Google
3️⃣ Google asks permission
4️⃣ Google sends access token to the app
5️⃣ App uses token to access user info

User → Google Login → Authorization → Access Token → App
