3️⃣ Access Token vs Refresh Token
Token Type	Lifetime	Purpose
Access Token	Short-lived (minutes to hours)	Access APIs/resources
Refresh Token	Long-lived (days to months)	Get new access tokens without re-login

Flow:

User logs in → receives access token + refresh token

Access token used for API requests

When access token expires → client sends refresh token to get a new access token
Access Token vs Refresh Token in OAuth 2.0 🔐

In OAuth2 authentication systems, two important tokens are used:

Access Token

Refresh Token

They work together to provide secure and seamless authentication.

1️⃣ Access Token

An access token is used by the client to access protected resources (APIs).

Characteristics

Short-lived (minutes to hours)

Sent with every API request

Usually implemented as JSON Web Token

Contains user claims (ID, role, permissions)

Example Request
GET /api/account/balance
Authorization: Bearer ACCESS_TOKEN
Example Payload
{
  "sub": "user123",
  "role": "CUSTOMER",
  "exp": 1710000000
}
2️⃣ Refresh Token

A refresh token is used to generate a new access token when the old one expires, without requiring the user to log in again.

Characteristics

Long-lived (days to months)

Sent only to the authorization server

Not used for accessing APIs directly

Often stored securely (database or secure cookie)

Example Refresh Request
POST /oauth/token
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=refresh_token
refresh_token=REFRESH_TOKEN
client_id=xyz

Server response:

{
  "access_token": "NEW_ACCESS_TOKEN",
  "expires_in": 3600
}
3️⃣ Why Both Tokens Are Needed

If only access tokens were used:

❌ Users would need to log in frequently

If only long-lived tokens were used:

❌ High security risk if stolen

Solution:

✔ Short-lived access tokens
✔ Long-lived refresh tokens

