A refresh token is used to obtain a new access token when the current one expires, without requiring the user to log in again. This provides both better security and better user experience.

1️⃣ Improved Security 🔐

Access tokens are short-lived (for example 10–15 minutes).

If an attacker steals an access token:

It will expire quickly

Damage is limited

Refresh tokens allow issuing new access tokens without making the access token long-lived.

2️⃣ Better User Experience 👤

Without refresh tokens:

Access Token expires → User must log in again

With refresh tokens:

Access Token expires → Client uses Refresh Token → New Access Token issued

Users stay logged in longer without repeated authentication.

3️⃣ Reduced Login Requests

Refresh tokens reduce the number of authentication operations.

Example:

Without refresh tokens:

User logs in every 15 minutes

With refresh tokens:

User logs in once → refresh token maintains session

This reduces load on authentication servers.

4️⃣ Enables Secure Long Sessions

Refresh tokens allow applications to maintain long user sessions securely.

Example:

Token	Lifetime
Access Token	15 minutes
Refresh Token	7 days

The system stays secure while allowing persistent login.

5️⃣ Supports Token Rotation 🔄

Refresh tokens can be rotated.

Flow:

Refresh Token → Request new access token
               ↓
Server returns new Access Token + new Refresh Token

If a refresh token is stolen, rotation helps detect misuse.

6️⃣ Works Well with Microservices

In microservice architectures:

Access tokens are used by APIs

Refresh tokens are handled by the authorization server

This keeps APIs stateless and scalable.

7️⃣ Real Banking Example 🏦

Mobile banking app:

1️⃣ User logs in once
2️⃣ Server issues:

Access Token → valid for 15 minutes

Refresh Token → valid for 7 days

3️⃣ When the access token expires:

App → Authorization Server
    → sends Refresh Token
    → receives new Access Token

User continues using the app without logging in again.

🎯 Interview Answer (Short)

Refresh tokens allow applications to obtain new access tokens without requiring users to log in again. They improve security by keeping access tokens short-lived while maintaining long user sessions. Refresh tokens also reduce authentication load and support secure token rotation.

