1️⃣ HTTPS (Secure Communication)

HTTPS is HTTP over TLS encryption, ensuring that communication between client and server is secure.

Why HTTPS is Important

Without HTTPS:

Client → HTTP → Server

Data can be intercepted (Man-in-the-Middle attack).

With HTTPS:

Client → Encrypted TLS → Server

Data is encrypted.

Benefits

✅ Encrypts data in transit
✅ Prevents data tampering
✅ Protects authentication tokens (JWT, OAuth tokens)

Example API call:

https://api.bank.com/account/balance
2️⃣ CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing)

CORS controls which domains are allowed to access your API.

Example problem:

Frontend: https://app.example.com
API: https://api.example.com

Browsers block cross-origin requests unless allowed.

CORS response headers:

Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://app.example.com
Access-Control-Allow-Methods: GET, POST
Access-Control-Allow-Headers: Authorization
Spring Boot Example
@Configuration
public class CorsConfig {

    @Bean
    public WebMvcConfigurer corsConfigurer() {
        return new WebMvcConfigurer() {
            public void addCorsMappings(CorsRegistry registry) {
                registry.addMapping("/api/**")
                        .allowedOrigins("https://app.example.com")
                        .allowedMethods("GET", "POST", "PUT", "DELETE");
            }
        };
    }
}
3️⃣ CSRF (Cross-Site Request Forgery)

CSRF occurs when a malicious website tricks a user into making unwanted requests.

Example attack:

User logged into bank
↓
User visits malicious site
↓
Malicious site sends request
POST /transfer-money

Bank server thinks the request is legitimate.

Protection Methods

✔ CSRF Tokens
✔ SameSite cookies
✔ Use JWT in Authorization header

Spring Security example:

http.csrf().disable();

(Usually disabled for stateless JWT APIs)

4️⃣ Rate Limiting

Rate limiting restricts the number of API requests a client can make.

Example:

Max 100 requests per minute per user

This prevents:

❌ DDoS attacks
❌ API abuse
❌ brute-force login attempts

Common algorithms:

Algorithm	Description
Token Bucket	Allows burst traffic
Leaky Bucket	Smooths traffic flow
Fixed Window	Simple request counting
Sliding Window	More accurate rate control

Example response:

HTTP/1.1 429 Too Many Requests
