API Access Continues – Client uses new access token to continue calling APIs.

When building secure backend APIs (Spring Boot, microservices, etc.), developers follow a set of security best practices to prevent attacks and protect user data.

1️⃣ Always Use HTTPS

Use HTTPS instead of HTTP to encrypt communication.

Benefits:

Prevents Man-in-the-Middle attacks

Protects credentials and tokens

Ensures data integrity

Example:

https://api.example.com/users

Never expose APIs over plain HTTP in production.

2️⃣ Use Strong Authentication

Use modern authentication methods like:

JSON Web Token

OAuth 2.0

API Keys (for internal services)

Example:

Authorization: Bearer JWT_TOKEN

Avoid storing passwords or secrets in APIs.

3️⃣ Implement Proper Authorization

Authentication verifies who the user is, but authorization controls what they can access.

Example roles:

Role	Permission
Admin	Manage users
Customer	View own account
Auditor	Read-only reports

Always validate permissions before executing operations.

4️⃣ Validate All Inputs

Never trust user input.

Protect against:

SQL Injection

Command Injection

XSS attacks

Example validation:

@NotNull
@Size(min = 3, max = 20)
private String username;

Also use prepared statements for database queries.

5️⃣ Implement Rate Limiting

Restrict the number of requests per client.

Example:

100 requests per minute per user

Benefits:

Prevents brute-force attacks

Protects APIs from abuse

Reduces server overload

Common algorithm:

Token Bucket

6️⃣ Use Secure Token Storage

Store authentication tokens securely:

Platform	Storage Method
Web Apps	HTTP-only cookies
Mobile Apps	Secure storage
Servers	Encrypted database

Avoid storing tokens in localStorage when possible.

7️⃣ Set Token Expiration

Always set expiration for tokens.

Example:

Token	Lifetime
Access Token	15 minutes
Refresh Token	7 days

Short-lived tokens reduce risk if stolen.

8️⃣ Implement Logging and Monitoring

Track security events like:

Login attempts

Token usage

Failed authentication

Example tools:

ELK Stack

Prometheus

Cloud monitoring tools

Logs help detect suspicious activity.

